Direct solar hydrogen generation (DSTH) is a promising approach for producing low-cost renewable hydrogen since the energy from the sun can be used to directly drive the water splitting redox reactions, eliminating the need for separate power generation and electrolyzer systems, as well as complex power infrastructure and additional balance of plant. Technoeconomic analysis of different direct solar hydrogen systems has identified that increasing the solar-to-hydrogen (STH) efficiency is important for reducing the levelized cost of hydrogen (LCOH).[4] For this reason, the US department of energy (DOE) has set a target STH efficiency of \(20\%\) by 2020 using low-cost materials to ensure economic viability of
large-scale direct solar hydrogen production.[5] While high STH efficiencies of up to \(19\%\) have already been reported, the systems invariably rely on the use of expensive semiconductors and/or noble-metal catalysts, reducing their economic viability for practical solar hydrogen generation.[6–9]
Among various approaches explored for DSTH, systems that physically separate the solar power conversion and the electrocatalytic water splitting achieve superior performance both in terms
of efficiency and stability. These systems use photovoltaic (PV) cells wired to electrodes and can leverage existing advances in solar cell technology. Due to relatively high voltage requirements of the water splitting reaction, two or more PV cells are generally required. Various low-cost PV cells and earth-abundant catalytic systems have been explored recently. For example, Luo et al. reported an STH efficiency of \(12.3\%\) using bifunctional NiFe catalysts and two series-connected perovskite solar cells,[10] while Schuttaf et al. reported \(14.2\%\) STH efficiency using three series-connected bifacial Si heterojunction cells paired with Ni-based catalysts.[11] Unfortunately, the STH efficiency of these series connected single-junction PV based systems is limited due to low operating current densities. Another approach is to use two PV cells with complementary bandgaps in a tandem configuration. In particular, perovskite-Si tandems have emerged as a promising low-cost alternative to III–V cells, with a record power conversion efficiency of \(29.1\%\) .[12] The International Technology Roadmap for Photovoltaics predicts that perovskite-Si tandems will be in mass production as early as 2021, paving the way for low cost, high efficiency tandem cells in the near future.[13] This makes them a preferable choice for large-scale implementation of solar hydrogen generation. Initial results for solar hydrogen generation using perovskite-Si tandems have shown promising STH efficiencies reaching up to \(18\%\) .[6,9]
The development of low-cost, earth-abundant metal-based electrocatalysts is critical to increasing the overall STH efficiency, thereby reducing the LCOH. The first-row transition metals, such as Ni, Fe, Co, have been demonstrated with good hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) and oxygen evolution reaction (OER) performances.[14–22] In particular, Ni alloys with other transition metals have received extensive attention due to their excellent HER/OER activities and stability under harsh alkaline conditions.[15–17,19,20] Ni alloyed with Mo is a well-known non-noble metal-based electrocatalyst for HER under alkaline condition.[17,23,24] Among Ni-based OER catalysts, NiFe (oxy) hydroxide presents a commendable performance for OER.[25–27] Although Ni-based catalysts have shown promise, their performance and operational stability need to be further improved, in order to match with PV cells, and construct robust, high-efficiency direct water splitting systems. The performance of an electrocatalyst is highly dependent on its morphology; a well-designed nanostructure not only can promote increased number of accessible active sites, but also could facilitate efficient mass/charge transfer and fast gas bubble dissipation, resulting in an enhanced catalytic efficiency.[28]
In this work, we present an integrated water splitting cell with a record STH efficiency under one sun illumination. We report a NiMo HER electrode with a "flower-stem" morphology coupled with a NiFe OER electrode exhibiting a low overpotential of \(1.48\;\mathrm{V}\) at \(10\;\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) and a remarkable stability of over \(100\;\mathrm{h}\) for overall water splitting. The unique "flower-stem" structure of the NiMo catalyst provides a hierarchical architecture for uniform dispersion of highly active NiMo alloy nanograins, thereby maximizing its catalytic performance. We develop an optimized bandgap \((1.72\;\mathrm{eV})\) quadruple perovskite solar cells passivated with large organic cation (n-dodecylammonium bromide) possessing a long alkyl chain length, resulting in a high open circuit voltage \((V_{\mathrm{oc}})\) of \(1.271\;\mathrm{V}\) and a power conversion efficiency (PCE) of \(24.3\%\) when paired with a monocrystalline n-Si cell
in a series-connected two-terminal (2-T) tandem configuration. Upon combining the tandem PV and catalyst electrodes with wired connections, the integrated system achieves a record STH efficiency of \(20\%\) with low-cost materials. With the help of STH calculations and technoeconomic analysis, we show that this STH efficiency could be further improved by realistic improvements in the perovskite solar cell performance and our system could enable low-cost solar hydrogen production.
## 2. Results and Discussion
## 2.1. Synthesis and Characterization of Electrocatalysts
NiMo catalysts for HER were prepared on nickel foam (NF) using a two-step method involving hydrothermal synthesis and calcination in a reducing environment. As shown in the scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images (Figure 1a,b), high density flower-like nanosheets and stem-like nanowires resembling flower-stem morphology grow uniformly on the 3D NF skeleton. Such morphology can improve electrolyte–catalyst interaction through capillary forces and reduce the adhesion of gas phase products, thereby enhancing the reaction kinetics.[29] The flower-like nanosheets are closely connected to the surface of the Ni foam through the stem-shaped nanowires, providing significantly enhanced accessible surface area with good mass and electron transfer properties. The SEM images of the samples taken immediately after hydrothermal synthesis show similar morphology with a smooth surface (Figure S1, Supporting Information). However, after \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) treatment, homogeneous nanograins are observed on the surface of the nanosheets and nanowires (Figure 1c,d). Calcination in a reducing environment is expected to form NiMo particles on the catalyst surface, which serve as active sites for HER.[17,24]
The energy-dispersive X-ray (EDX) elemental mapping and the spectrum of low-magnification SEM image presented in Figure S2 in the Supporting Information confirm an even distribution of Ni, Mo, O in NiMo/NF catalyst. The transmission electron microscopy (TEM) (Figure S3, Supporting Information) analysis confirms that both the flower-like and stem-like structures (rolled nanosheet) are composed of hollow nanosheets. The thickness of the nanosheets is found to be \(\sim1\;\mathrm{nm}\) from atomic force microscopy analysis (Figure S4, Supporting Information). The interplanar spacings of the lattice fringes determined from the high-angle annular dark-field scanning transmission electron microscopy (HAADF–STEM) image (Figure 1e) are 2.0 and \(2.4\;\mathrm{\AA}\), which are assigned to the (211) plane of \(\mathrm{MoNi}_{4}\) and (200) plane of \(\mathrm{MoO}_{2}\), respectively. The fast Fourier transform (FFT) pattern shown in Figure 1f indicates a mixed chemical composition of \(\mathrm{MoNi}_{4}\) ((130) and (211)), \(\mathrm{MoO}_{2}\) ((200) and (011)), and \(\mathrm{NiMoO}_{4}\) ((220) and (110)), which is further confirmed by the X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns (Figure S5, Supporting Information). It can also be seen from the XRD patterns of the catalyst before and after \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) calcination that the \(\mathrm{MoNi}_{4}\) phase is formed only after the reduction treatment. The EDX mapping of TEM with high magnification \((5\;\mathrm{nm})\) presented in Figure 1g on a nanoparticle on the stem-like structure shows that the NiMo alloy (Ni rich) is encapsulated by NiMo oxides. It further confirms the formation of NiMo grains
in a reducing environment. The surface chemical composition of the NiMo/NF was analyzed by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS). The deconvoluted peaks of the Mo 3D spectrum at 2279/231.4, 229.3/232.5, 230.4/233.6, and 232.2/235.4 eV can be fitted to the binding energies of \(\mathrm{Mo}^{0}\), \(\mathrm{Mo}^{4+}\), \(\mathrm{Mo}^{5+}\) and \(\mathrm{Mo}^{6+}\), respectively (Figure 1h).[20] Figure 1i shows the deconvolution of Ni \(2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\) spectrum of NiMo/NF which demonstrates a sharp peak at \(852.5~\mathrm{eV}\) representing \(\mathrm{Ni}^{0}\). The peaks at 854.4, 855.7, and \(856.9~\mathrm{eV}\) are assigned to \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) while the peak at \(858.7~\mathrm{eV}\) is attributed to \(\mathrm{Ni}^{3+}\), and satellite peaks have binding energies of 861.9 and \(864.4~\mathrm{eV}\).[31,32] These results confirm the coexistence of \(\mathrm{MoNi}_{4}\) alloy with various NiMo oxide compounds.
To pair with NiMo/NF, we prepared NiFe/NF electrodes for OER using electrodeposition method, producing vertically grown NiFe nanoneedles completely covering the Ni foam
surface (Figure S6a-c, Supporting Information). The TEM (Figure S6d,e, Supporting Information) and XRD analyses (Figure S7, Supporting Information) of NiFe/NF confirm that the NiFe catalyst consists of ultrathin amorphous nanosheets. From the XPS analysis of NiFe/NF (Figure S8, Supporting Information), the Fe 2p and Ni 2p spectra show highly oxidized states \((\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}/\mathrm{Ni}^{3+}\) and \(\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}/\mathrm{Fe}^{3+})\), which are consistent with previously reported results and the high oxidized chemical states are favorable for oxidation reactions.[26]
## 2.2. Evaluation of Electrochemical Performance for Water Splitting
After synthesizing the electrocatalysts, we tested their electrochemical performance in 1 m KOH in both three-electrode and
two-electrode configurations to evaluate the individual HER and OER performances and the combined overall water splitting performance, respectively. For benchmarking purposes, we also prepared Pt-C/NF and Ir-C/NF electrodes following commonly reported synthesis procedures as detailed in the Experimental Section. As shown in Figure 2a, the as-synthesized NiMo/NF presents an HER onset potential close to the thermodynamic potential, and low overpotentials of 6 and \(70\;\mathrm{mV}\) to obtain the current densities of 10 and \(100\;\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\). In comparison, the benchmark Pt-C/NF and unmodified NF show much higher overpotentials of 130 and \(270\;\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) at 10 and \(100\;\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\), respectively. The Tafel slope obtained for NiMo/NF (78 mV dec\(^{-1}\)) is substantially lower than that of Pt-C/NF (86 mV dec\(^{-1}\)) and NF (110 mV dec\(^{-1}\)), suggesting rapid reaction kinetics. The HER activity of NiMo/NF is also much superior to NiMoO/NF (Figure S9, Supporting Information). The double layer capacitance of NiMo/NF was measured to be \(9.8\;\mathrm{mF}\;\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\), which is five times higher than NiMoO/NF (Figure S10, Supporting Information). These results confirm the surface \(\mathrm{MoNi}_{4}\) nanograins formed after the reduction process supply abundant electrocatalytic active sites for HER. Moreover, the NiMo/NF electrode exhibits excellent longstanding stability, maintaining a stable HER activity at a high current density of \(100\;\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) after \(100\;\mathrm{h}\) of continuous operation (Figure 2b). Such a performance ranks NiMo/NF catalyst among the best non-noble metal HER catalysts in alkaline media (Figure 2c; Table S1, Supporting Information).
On the other hand, the NiFe/NF electrode presents a low overpotential of \(255\;\mathrm{mV}\) at \(10\;\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) and small Tafel slope (30 mV dec\(^{-1}\)) for OER, outperforming the benchmark Ir-C/NF and pristine NF. The overpotential obtained from the LSV curve matches closely with that observed from continuous chronopotentiometry (Figure S11a, Supporting Information) confirming there is no influence of the oxidation peak at \(1.42\;\mathrm{V}\) on the reported over potential. To obtain a current density of \(100\;\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\), the NiFe/NF requires a low overpotential of \(299\;\mathrm{mV}\), which is considerably lower than the overpotential needed by Ir-C/NF and NF. The performance of NiFe/NF in the current study is comparable or superior to many other recently developed transition metal OER electrocatalysts.[33,34]
The overall water splitting was evaluated in a two-electrode configuration in 1 m KOH by employing NiFe/NF and NiMo/NF as anode and cathode, respectively. Figure 2e shows that the NiFe/NF||NiMo/NF delivers \(10\;\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) current at a voltage of \(1.48\;\mathrm{V}\), which is 80 and \(230\;\mathrm{mV}\) better than Ir-C/NF||Pt-C/NF and NF||NF, respectively. Besides the excellent activity, the stability of NiFe/NF||NiMo/NF is also superior to Ir-C/NF||Pt-C/NF, showing no degradation at \(20\;\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) after \(100\;\mathrm{h}\) operation while significant activity loss is observed for Ir-C/NF||Pt-C/NF within \(20\;\mathrm{h}\) (Figure 2f). Compared with the linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) curve for the as-prepared catalysts, the LSV curve taken after \(100\;\mathrm{h}\) stability shows negligible change in overpotentials (Figure S11b, Supporting Information). Moreover, the gas measurement was performed on both the
electrodes. The Faradaic efficiencies are calculated to be \(99.7\%\) for NiMo/NF electrode (Table S2 and Figure S12a, Supporting Information) and \(99.3\%\) for NiFe/NF electrode (Figure S12b, Supporting Information), respectively, suggesting there are no side reactions other than the hydrogen evolution and oxygen evolution reactions at the respective electrodes. In summary, the electrochemical performance tests confirm the as-developed NiFe/NF||NiMo/NF electrodes form a highly active and robust catalytic system for overall water splitting.
## 2.3. Perovskite-Si Tandem PV for Water Splitting
To achieve stand-alone solar hydrogen production in an integrated system using the Ni-based electrodes, we fabricated perovskite and Si cells in two-terminal (2-T) tandem configuration. Figure 3a illustrates the device structure of the tandem cell consisting of a semitransparent perovskite solar cell and a monocrystalline n-type Si solar cell. The perovskite cell bandgap is optimized at \(1.72\ \mathrm{eV}\), which is close to the ideal top-cell bandgap of \(\approx1.7\ \mathrm{eV}\) to achieve current matching with the Si bottom cell in the tandem configuration and to obtain the maximum STH efficiency.[35–37] Semitransparent perovskite solar cells were fabricated using a 3D perovskite light
absorber \((\mathrm{Rb}_{0.05}\mathrm{Cs}_{0.095}\mathrm{MA}_{0.1425}\mathrm{FA}_{0.7125}\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\mathrm{Br})\). To realize high photovoltage, the surface of the active perovskite layer was passivated with a large organic cation \(n\)-dodecylammonium bromide \((\mathrm{C}_{12}\mathrm{H}_{28}\mathrm{BrN})\) consisting of a long alkyl chain, which is known to form 2D perovskites.[38] The organic cation with long alkyl chain presents better surface passivation compared to the previously reported \(n\)-butylammonium bromide \((\mathrm{C}_{4}\mathrm{H}_{12}\mathrm{BrN})\) which has a much shorter alkyl chain. This is evidenced by a significantly higher photoluminescence (PL) intensity and carrier lifetime in the \(n\)-dodecylammonium bromide passivated perovskite film as compared to the bare perovskite film and perovskite film passivated by \(n\)-butylammonium bromide (Figure S13, Supporting Information).
In our previous report, we demonstrated that coating perovskite films with \(n\)-butylammonium bromide leads to the formation of a 2D Ruddlesden–Popper (RP) phase. The 2D layer at the surface of the 3D perovskite passivates the surface defects, changes the electronic structure, and thus result in improved device performance.[39] The length of the alkyl chain has a strong influence on the packing density, desorption energy, and carrier dynamics of ligands in the 2D layer.[40,41] Since the \(n\)-dodecylammonium bromide has a much longer alkyl chain, it is expected to further change the electronic structure at the surface of 3D perovskite. This results in a more favorable extraction of holes
from the perovskite to the hole transport layer Spiro-MeOTAD and improved electron blocking property of the passivation layer.[42] In addition, the use of a long alkyl chain organic cation on the perovskite surface substantially increases the hydrophobicity of the perovskite film. As shown in Figure S14 in the Supporting Information, the contact angle of perovskite film to water increases from \(52^{\circ}\) to \(88^{\circ}\) upon passivating the perovskite film with \(n\)-dodecylammonium bromide, which could potentially lead to enhanced moisture stability of perovskite solar cells. Figure S15 in the Supporting Information shows the top-view and cross-sectional SEM images of the perovskite cell, where the semitransparent rear contact is comprised of a \(\mathrm{MoO}_{3}\) buffer layer and a indium doped zinc oxide (ZO) contact with a thickness of 7 and \(40\;\mathrm{nm}\), respectively. Area matched bottom Si cells were fabricated with rear n-doped electron-selective passivation layer.[43]
The current density-voltage \((J\!\!-\!\!V)\) characteristics of the semitransparent perovskite top cell and the silicon bottom cell were evaluated under continuous AM1.5G illumination (Figure 3b). The efficiency of the semitransparent perovskite cell is \(15.3\%\), with an open circuit potential \((V_{\mathrm{oc}})\) of \(1.271\;\mathrm{V}\), a short circuit current density \((J_{\mathrm{sc}})\) of \(178\;\mathrm{mA\,cm}^{-2}\), and a fill factor (FF) of \(68\%\). It is worth noting that the \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) exhibited by the semitransparent perovskite cell is the highest open circuit voltage for large area \((>\!1\;\mathrm{cm}^{2})\) semitransparent perovskite solar cells ever reported (Table S3, Supporting Information). The \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\), deficit of \(0.449\;\mathrm{V}\) is also the lowest value reported for this type of devices. This achievement can be ascribed to the excellent passivation property of the 2D perovskite formed by \(n\)-dodecylammonium bromide used in this work. Si cell with an initial PCE of \(21.8\%\), \(J_{\mathrm{sc}}\) of \(38.72\;\mathrm{mA\,cm}^{-2}\), and \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) of \(0.699\;\mathrm{V}\), presents a PCE of \(10.1\%\), \(J_{\mathrm{sc}}\) of \(18.8\;\mathrm{mA\,cm}^{-2}\), and \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) of \(0.675\;\mathrm{V}\) under the perovskite cell. When the perovskite and Si cells were series-connected in 2-T tandem configuration, the tandem cell achieves a \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) of \(1.92\;\mathrm{V}\), a \(J_{\mathrm{sc}}\) of \(17.6\;\mathrm{mA\,cm}^{-2}\) and a FF of \(72.5\%\), corresponding to a PCE of \(24.3\%\), when measured through a \(1\,\mathrm{cm}\times1\,\mathrm{cm}\) aperture mask.
To determine the spectral response of the tandem system, absorption and transmission response of the perovskite cells were measured (Figure S16a, Supporting Information). The maximum obtainable electron flux values for the perovskite cell and Si cell under the perovskite cell are calculated using their respective external quantum efficiency (EQE) data (Figure S14b, Supporting Information) and incident photon flux as shown in Figure 3c. This translates to calculated photocurrents of 170 and \(18.0\;\mathrm{mA\,cm}^{-2}\) for the perovskite and Si cells, respectively, which match closely with the \(J_{\mathrm{sc}}\) values obtained from \(J\!\!-\!\!V\) curves. The electron flux data shows that the two subcells possess a close current matching with little room to improve. Additionally, the perovskite cell tested in an \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) environment shows excellent stability up to \(100\;\mathrm{h}\) (Figure 3d). Suitably encapsulated perovskite cells are expected to show similar stability under real world operating conditions. This result, together with the stability results of the Ni-based electrodes, verifies that the low-cost material system presented in this work can be made into a durable water splitting system.
## 2.4. Direct Solar-To-Hydrogen Generation
Figure 4a illustrates the integrated water splitting system consisting of perovskite-Si PV wire-connected with catalytic
electrodes. The operating performance of the integrated system can be calculated by overlaying the \(J\!\!-\!\!V\) curve of the series connected perovskite-Si tandem cell with the LSV curve of the two-electrode catalyst system as shown in Figure 4b. The intersection point indicates an operating current density \((J_{\mathrm{op}})\) of \(16.27\;\mathrm{mA\,cm}^{-2}\) for solar-driven water splitting. The STH conversion efficiency of the integrated system can be calculated using the following equation
\[
\mathrm{STH}=\left[\frac{\left(1.23-V_{\mathrm{app}}\right)\times J_{\mathrm{op}}\times\eta_{\mathrm{f}}}{P_{\mathrm{sc}}}\right]_{\mathrm{AM1.5G}}
\]
where, \(V_{\mathrm{app}}\) is the applied potential which is equal to zero in this case. \(1.23\;\mathrm{V}\) is the reversible potential required for water splitting reaction at \(25\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). \(\eta_{\mathrm{f}}\) is the faradic efficiency and \(P_{\mathrm{in}}\) is the incident irradiation power \((100\;\mathrm{mW\,m}^{-2})\). The gas measurements (Figure S17, Supporting Information) conducted using continuous-flow gas chromatography in a two-electrode system with NiMo/NF and NiFe/NF electrodes confirm that both the electrodes are faradaic \((\eta_{\mathrm{f}}=1)\). Moreover, the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{:O}_{2}\) ratio is found to be \(\approx2:1\) for the duration of measurements with no side reactions. Thus, the integrated system achieves a remarkable STH efficiency of \(20.0\%\) for unassisted solar water splitting.
Furthermore, the system shows excellent stability with no noticeable degradation after \(15\;\mathrm{h}\) of continuous operation under AM1.5G illumination (Figure 4c). The current fluctuation observed during the long operation is attributable to bubbles formation on the electrode surfaces. The integrated system assessed under chopped illumination shows a rapid photoresponse (inset of Figure 4c). A systematic comparison of the STH efficiencies reported in the literature confirms the present work achieves the highest reported STH efficiency under 1 sun illumination (Figure 4d; Table S4, Supporting Information). It is noteworthy that a number of previous demonstrations relied on expensive III–V semiconductors and/or noble-metal catalysts for achieving higher STH efficiencies, while this work leverages commercially relevant tandem solar cells and earth-abundant catalyst materials. This record STH efficiency is achieved by realising significant improvements in the performance of the Ni-based catalysts and the perovskite PV cell, as well as optimising the integration of the system as a whole.
## 2.5. STH Efficiency and Cost Analysis, and Pathways for Low-Cost Solar Hydrogen
The STH efficiency of the integrated system was modelled using previously reported methods to understand further efficiency enhancements that can be realistically achieved.[37,44] The photovoltaic component is modelled using the transcendental solar cell equation. Losses are included as a function of various loss parameters; parasitic absorption, optical losses, series resistance \((\mathrm{R}_{\mathrm{S}})\) and external radiative efficiency (ERE) to include non-ideal behavior as shown in Figure 5a. The catalytic component was modelled using Nernst and Butler–Volmer equation. Details of the STH efficiency model including the fitting procedure for realistic systems and parameter values are described in the Supporting Information.
The current record PCE of \( 29\% \) for perovskite-Si tandem cells significantly surpasses the record single junction Si cell efficiency of \( 26.7\% \).[12,45] Yet, it represents only \( 64\% \) of the detailed balance efficiency limit.[35] With Si solar cell efficiencies nearing practically achievable limits, there is a greater chance of improving the tandem PV and STH efficiencies by improving the perovskite cell efficiency. Therefore, we examined realistic improvements in perovskite solar cell performance estimated from experimental demonstrations in literature as well as theoretical projections.[46,47] Currently, the performance of perovskite solar cells is limited by low fill factor due to high series resistance.[48] Recent improvements in low bandgap perovskite solar cells show the fill factor of large bandgap semitransparent perovskite cells can be greatly improved.[49,50] Reducing the series resistance close to absolute 0 (here 0.07) \(\Omega\,\mathrm{cm}^{2}\)[46] improves the fill factor to \( 85\% \), increases the PCE by \( 3.8\% \) and STH by \( 1.7\% \) absolute.
Improvement in the \( V_{\mathrm{oc}} \) of perovskite cells with a given bandgap occurs primarily from reduced recombination. Recent reports of large \( V_{\mathrm{oc}} \) for high bandgap perovskite cells suggest that improvements are realistically possible for semitransparent cells.[51–53] Increasing the \( V_{\mathrm{oc}} \) would result in considerable improvement in the PCE by \( 2.2\% \), but it does not further improve the STH efficiency due to a fixed voltage requirement for solar water splitting.
Optical losses and parasitic absorption in the perovskite cells are crucial factors influencing the 2-T tandem PV efficiency. Realistic improvement in the optical performance of the perovskite solar cells is difficult to predict but the learning curve of Si and GaAs cells[45,54] and recent improvements in perovskite solar cells suggest that absorption above \( 95\% \) could be achieved, while minimizing parasitic absorption.[55] Our model indicates that improving the incoming irradiation collection efficiency to \( 95\% \) increases the PCE by \( 2.2\% \) and the STH by \( 1.5\% \) absolute. High transparency and low resistivity contacts such as silver nanowires, graphene, and wire-metal-oxide hybrids, demonstrate that the parasitic absorption can be reduced to around \( 5\% \).[56] This results in improvement of the current densities of the individual cells and hence the tandem PV, increasing the PCE by \( 1.9\% \) and the STH efficiency by \( 1.6\% \).
Cumulatively, optimizing the top perovskite cell can improve the 2-T perovskite Si tandem solar cell efficiency to \( 35\% \) consistent with the previous reports.[46] This results in a realistically achievable STH efficiency close to \( 25\% \) based on the Si cell and catalytic system presented in this work, confirming the potential to achieve the US DOE target of \( 25\% \) STH for the year 2025 for large-scale hydrogen generation.
Next, the LCOH produced by DSTH system is calculated and compared with an off-grid electrolyzer (OGE) system powered
Figure 5b shows a comparison of the LCOH and the cost breakdown for all the three cases. The LCOH for the current DSTH system is calculated to be \(\mathbb{S}4.1\;\mathrm{kg}^{-1}\). For the OGE system using commercial Si solar panels with \(18\%\) efficiency and an electrolyzer with \(61\%\) efficiency results in LCOH of \(\mathbb{S}7.4\;\mathrm{kg}^{-1}\). Replacing the Si modules with high efficiency tandem PV reported in this work, the LCOH can be reduced to \(\mathbb{S}6.6\;\mathrm{kg}^{-1}\). Figure 5c shows the LCOH as a function of the STH efficiency, assuming all the component costs remain the same, and demonstrates that increasing the STH efficiency of the current system to \(25\%\) can reduce the LCOH to \(\mathbb{S}3.3\;\mathrm{kg}^{-1}\). This implies that realistic improvements in the perovskite cell alone could provide a pathway to significantly reduce the LCOH in the future.
Finally, we consider the effect of the cost of different components in the integrated system on the estimated LCOH for STH efficiency \(20\%\). The tandem PV panel and membrane costs represent significant fractions of \(23\%\) and \(16.5\%\) of the overall costs, respectively. Perovskite-Si tandems are not yet commercially available, and there is significant uncertainty about the lower limit of tandem module costs once production moves down the learning curve. Claimed to be a low-cost technology and still under development, there are still possibilities to achieve lower perovskite cell costs.[58] The lower limit of the cost of perovskite-Si tandems is taken to be equal to the cost of a standard silicon module, while the upper limit is assumed to be \(40\%\) higher. Further, membrane cost is estimated based on current production values. Following the learning curve of other technologies, membrane cost is expected to decrease significantly when produced on large-scale. Figure 5d shows the impact of the costs of the tandem PV module and membrane on the LCOH, assuming an STH efficiency of \(20\%\). This shows that LCOH below \(\mathbb{S}3\;\mathrm{kg}^{-1}\) can be achieved for membrane cost of \(\mathbb{S}15\;\mathrm{m}^{-2}\) and tandem PV module costs of \(\mathbb{S}49.9\;\mathrm{m}^{-2}\), equal to current Si module cost. Combining the projected efficiency enhancements with the projected cost reductions discussed above, the LCOH reduces to \(\mathbb{S}2.3\;\mathrm{kg}^{-1}\) (Figure S18, Supporting Information).
Direct solar water splitting is a developing technology and there is no data on long-term operation of such systems, hence there could be possible variations from the current assumptions. Nevertheless, our calculations show promising LCOH projections, assuming that improvements in the performance of low-cost solar hydrogen systems continue to occur, and realistic cost reductions in components are achieved as the manufacturing expands. In summary, the current system achieves a substantial efficiency milestone based on low-cost materials and demonstrates potential to accomplish STH efficiency close to \(25\%\), desired operational stability, and competitive LCOH, thereby promising its large-scale application.
## 3. Conclusions
High efficiency direct solar water splitting systems made of low-cost light-absorbers and catalysts are a promising pathway to enable commercialization of solar hydrogen technologies. We have demonstrated a direct solar water splitting system with \(20\%\) STH efficiency by coupling earth-abundant catalyst electrodes with high-performance perovskite-Si tandem cells, fulfilling the STH efficiency target set by the DOE for the year 2020. To achieve this, we have developed NiMo electrodes with a high density of NiMo active sites in flower-stem morphology, exhibiting an exceptional HRR performance, and an improved perovskite top cell with a record open circuit voltage achieved using \(n\)-dodecylammonium bromide. In addition, we have assessed the potential for further improvement in STH efficiency and cost competitiveness of solar hydrogen production using the reported low-cost material system. Our analysis shows that an LCOH of \(\\(4.1\,\mathsf{kg}^{-1}\) can be achieved with current cost estimates based on an STH efficiency of \(20\%\). Improving solely the perovskite cell could enhance the STH efficiency close to \(25\%\). Combined with future reductions in the PV panel and membrane costs, an LCOH of \(62.3\,\mathsf{kg}^{-1}\) could be achieved, presenting a remarkable opportunity to realize cheap renewable hydrogen.
## 4. Experimental Section
Materials: Ammonium molybdate tetrahydrate \(((\mathsf{N H}_{4})_{6}\mathsf{M o}_{7}\mathsf{O}_{24}\) \(4\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}\), \({>}99.98\%)\), nickel nitrate hexahydrate \((\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}\) \(6\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}\), \({>}98.5\%)\), iron nitrate nonahydrate \((\mathsf{F e}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}\) \(9\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}\), \({>}98\%)\), and Nafion \((5\,\,\mathsf{w t}\%\) in lower aliphatic alcohols and water) were purchased from Sigma-Aldrich. Formamidinium iodide, formamidinium bromide, methylammonium iodide, methylammonium bromide, \(n\)-BABr, iso-BABr, t-BABr, and \(\mathsf{T i O}_{2}\) paste were procured from GreatCell Solar Materials. Lead iodide was purchased from Alfa Aesar. Spiro-MeOTAD was purchased from Lumentech. Refractive index matching layer (series AA, refractive index \(=1.414\)) was obtained from SPI Supplies. Other materials were purchased from Sigma-Aldrich and used as received.
Synthesis of Electroanalysis: Before use, NF substrates were cleaned sequentially in \(3\,\mathrm{~m~}\) HCl solution, acetone, and deionized water in an ultrasonic water bath. NiMo/NF electrodes were fabricated by a modified hydrothermal and hydrogen reduction method. First, NF substrates \((2\,\mathsf{c m}\times4\,\mathsf{c m})\) were placed in a mixed aqueous solution of \(0.03\,\mathrm{~m~}\) \(((\mathsf{N H}_{4})_{6}\mathsf{M o}_{7}\mathsf{O}_{24}\) \(4\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}\) and \(0.12\,\mathrm{~m~}\) \((\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}\) \(6\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}\) in a \(25\,\mathsf{m L}\) Teflon-stainless steel reactor, followed by a hydrothermal reaction at \(150\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(15\,\mathrm{~h~}\) in an oven. The obtained NF sample was cleaned by deionized water and ethanol, and dried at \(70\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(5\,\mathrm{~h~}\), after which they were calcined in a vacuum furnace under the flow of \(10\,\mathsf{v o l}\%\) \(\mathsf{H}_{2}\) in Ar \((55\,\mathsf{m L}\,\mathsf{m i n}^{-1})\) for \(3\,\mathrm{~h~}\).
NiFe/NF electrodes were fabricated by electrodeposition in a mixed aqueous solution of \(3\times10^{-3}\) M \((\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}\) \(6\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}\) and \(3\times10^{-3}\) M \(\mathsf{F e}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{3}\) \(9\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}\) using a three-electrode system, with saturated calomel electrode (SCE), NF, and Pt plate as the reference, working, and counter electrodes, respectively. The electrodeposition process was carried out at a constant potential of \(-1.0\,\mathsf{V}\) (vs SCE) for \(300\,s\).
To prepare Pt-C/NF and Ir-C/NF electrodes, commercially available \(20\%\) platinum on carbon \((\mathsf{P t}/\mathsf{C})\) and \(20\%\) iridium on carbon \((\mathsf{I r}/\mathsf{C})\) powders purchased from fuel cell store were prepared into inks by sonicating \(5\,\mathsf{m g}\) powder with \(475\,\upmu\mathrm{L}\) ethanol, \(500\,\upmu\mathrm{L}\) distilled water and \(25\,\upmu\mathrm{L}\) Nafion. The uniformly mixed inks were drop casted on NF substrates and dried at room temperature to obtain Pt-C/NF and Ir-C/ NF electrodes.
corrected monochromated TEM, equipped with a SuperX EDS detector system for ultrahigh count rates, and operated at \(300~\mathrm{kV}\). The surface chemical properties were studied by XPS using a Thermo ESCALAB250i high-resolution X-ray photoelectron spectrometer. The reflectance and transmittance of perovskite solar cells were measured using a Lambda 1050 spectrophotometer (Perkin Elmer) equipped with an integrating sphere.
Electrochemical Characterizations: A typical three-electrode system composed of a working electrode, counter electrode (graphite rod for HER and Pt plate for OER), and reference electrode (SCE) was applied to evaluate the electrocatalytic HER or OER activity of the developed electrodes. The LSV curves were obtained in \(1\,\mathrm{~M~}\) KOH at a scan rate of \(5\,\mathrm{mV\,s^{-1}}\) under \(85\%\) iR compensation. The double layer capacitance was measured by cyclic voltammetry (CV) scan at scan rate range from 200 to \(600\;\mathrm{mV}\;\mathrm{s}^{-1}\). Faradaic efficiency of \(O_{2}\) production was evaluated by rotating ring disk electrode (RRDE). The NiFe catalyst was ultrasonically separated from NiFe/NF samples in ethanol solution and made into an ink to drop-cast on a glassy carbon disk (5 mm in diameter). Faradaic efficiency of hydrogen production was evaluated by quantifying the reaction products by an online gas chromatography (GC) equipped with a thermal conductivity detector (TCD). The overall water splitting performance was evaluated in a two-electrode system in \(1\,\mathrm{~M~}\) KOH at a scan rate of \(2\,\mathrm{mV\,s^{-1}}\). For continuous-flow gas measurements, the NiMo/NF and NiFe/NF electrodes were placed in two-electrode configuration in \(1\,\mathrm{~M~}\) KOH in an air tight electrochemical cell. The cell was purged with nitrogen under constant stirring overnight before the actual gas evolutions were recorded. The measurements were performed using Shimadzu GC2030 system.
PV and STH Characterizations: The \(J{-}V\) characteristics of the solar cells were assessed using a solar simulator (WAVELABS SINUS-220) equipped with a potentiostat (AutolabPGSTAT302N). The light intensity was calibrated at one Sun (100 mW \(\mathsf{c m}^{-2}\), AM1.5G) using a certified FraunhoferCalLab reference cell. Unless stated otherwise, the scan rate was fixed at \(50\,\mathrm{mV\,s^{-1}}\) with a voltage step of \(10\,\mathrm{mV}\) and dwell time of \(200\,\mathrm{ms}\). EOE spectra of the perovskite solar cell was obtained using a homemade setup without light bias in DC mode including a tungsten light source, a monochromator SP AB301-T, two Keithley 2425 sourcemeters, and a Si reference cell. The system was calibrated by the National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST)-certified reference Si and Ge photodiodes. For stability measurements, the cells were placed inside a temperature-controlled jig filled with constant flow of \(\mathsf{N}_{2}\). Direct solar-to-hydrogen measurements were conducted in a two-electrode system with NiFe/NF as the anode and NiMo/NF as the cathode in \(1\,\mathrm{~M~}\) KOH, with perovskite and cells connected in series. The unbiased currents generated were assessed using a potentiostat (AutolabPGSTAT302N).
